WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.055
The robot from lecture one is back.

2
00:00:06.145 --> 00:00:09.299
Its matrix M turns how many of each

3
00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.274
move into where the robot lands.

4
00:00:12.274 --> 00:00:15.969
Move a is one, three, and move b is two

5
00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:16.469
six.

6
00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.594
Two moves a, then one move b backwards

7
00:00:19.594 --> 00:00:22.490
and the robot is back where it started.

8
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.396
Two questions, for every target at once.

9
00:00:26.396 --> 00:00:28.884
Which targets can it reach?

10
00:00:28.884 --> 00:00:31.551
And which recipes go nowhere?

11
00:00:31.551 --> 00:00:34.840
Pause and picture the targets first.

12
00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:42.230
Take x moves a and y moves b

13
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:48.143
the robot lands at x times a plus y times b.

14
00:00:48.143 --> 00:00:50.508
But b is twice a

15
00:00:50.508 --> 00:00:56.420
so that is the same as x plus two y moves a.

16
00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.333
Every target is a multiple of one, three.

17
00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:04.070
Stretch the arrow forwards, and backwards

18
00:01:04.157 --> 00:01:06.503
and the targets fill a line

19
00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.241
through the origin.

20
00:01:08.241 --> 00:01:11.370
That line is the column space of M.

21
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.105
The target seven, twenty one is on it

22
00:01:15.105 --> 00:01:16.975
further up the line.

23
00:01:16.975 --> 00:01:19.737
The target seven, five is not

24
00:01:19.737 --> 00:01:22.320
no recipe at all reaches it.

25
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.270
A numerical test: put b next to the columns

26
00:01:26.348 --> 00:01:29.562
as an extra column, and compute the rank

27
00:01:29.562 --> 00:01:32.540
the number of independent directions.

28
00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:36.990
For seven, twenty one, and for minus two

29
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.090
minus six, the rank stays one

30
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:41.790
they are inside.

31
00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:45.790
For seven, five, the rank rises to two

32
00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.290
it is outside.

33
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.429
And the origin is always inside.

34
00:01:50.429 --> 00:01:52.520
Take every amount zero.

35
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.761
A common slip is to think the

36
00:01:55.852 --> 00:01:58.421
column space is the columns.

37
00:01:58.421 --> 00:02:01.265
Here are the two columns, one

38
00:02:01.265 --> 00:02:04.660
three and two, six: just two arrows.

39
00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.439
That is not the column space.

40
00:02:07.523 --> 00:02:09.952
It is the whole infinite line

41
00:02:10.036 --> 00:02:12.800
of their combinations: half of a

42
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.396
minus one a, two and a half a

43
00:02:15.396 --> 00:02:17.490
and every point between.

44
00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.770
Now the second question

45
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.358
and a different plane.

46
00:02:23.358 --> 00:02:26.058
Its points are amounts

47
00:02:26.058 --> 00:02:28.870
x moves a and y moves b.

48
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.397
The recipes seven, zero and five

49
00:02:32.397 --> 00:02:35.864
one both land at seven, twenty one.

50
00:02:35.864 --> 00:02:39.330
Their difference is two, minus one

51
00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.700
two a minus b, which goes nowhere.

52
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:47.394
M x equal to zero says x plus two y is zero.

53
00:02:47.394 --> 00:02:51.638
So every multiple of two, minus one works

54
00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:55.192
three times it, minus two times it.

55
00:02:55.192 --> 00:02:58.450
That line is the nullspace of M.

56
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.683
The recipe one, one is not on it

57
00:03:01.683 --> 00:03:05.440
it lands at three, nine, not at the start.

58
00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:06.686
Careful

59
00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:09.768
both answers are lines in the plane

60
00:03:09.768 --> 00:03:12.183
but in two different planes.

61
00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:14.599
The column space lives where

62
00:03:14.682 --> 00:03:18.180
the robot moves: its points are positions.

63
00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:22.505
The nullspace lives where the recipes live

64
00:03:22.505 --> 00:03:24.723
its points are amounts.

65
00:03:24.723 --> 00:03:28.421
For a matrix with m rows and n columns

66
00:03:28.421 --> 00:03:32.580
the column space has one entry per equation

67
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.370
and the nullspace one entry per unknown.

68
00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:40.984
Is the zero recipe always in the nullspace?

69
00:03:40.984 --> 00:03:43.516
Yes: A times zero is zero.

70
00:03:43.516 --> 00:03:47.550
Can the nullspace be only the zero vector?

71
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:48.297
Yes.

72
00:03:48.297 --> 00:03:50.442
The paint matrix of lecture

73
00:03:50.522 --> 00:03:52.190
two has three pivots

74
00:03:52.269 --> 00:03:54.494
so its only recipe that goes

75
00:03:54.573 --> 00:03:56.400
nowhere is zero itself.

76
00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.260
The line shrinks to a single point.

77
00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.087
So, the column space is every A x

78
00:04:03.087 --> 00:04:05.474
a subspace of positions.

79
00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.053
A x equals b can be solved

80
00:04:08.148 --> 00:04:10.440
exactly when b is in it.

81
00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:13.149
The nullspace is every x

82
00:04:13.247 --> 00:04:15.507
with A x equal to zero

83
00:04:15.507 --> 00:04:17.767
a subspace of amounts.

84
00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:20.813
For the robot, both are lines

85
00:04:20.813 --> 00:04:23.270
in two different planes.

86
00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:26.448
Your turn: is the target three

87
00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:28.729
nine in the column space

88
00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.827
of the robot's matrix?

89
00:04:30.827 --> 00:04:34.203
In part two, a drone flies in space

90
00:04:34.203 --> 00:04:36.940
and the lines become a plane.
